#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519984 :question-generator/id #uuid "1b5b1178-324b-5bad-aa78-0b6a6b3ba485" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179718.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814fc6cf-8415-51dc-aa71-2b2dcbf81962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254814} {:answer/id #uuid "86f05304-f4c1-54b6-a4c5-000eafd2bbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254865} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6b72a-eb09-5d1b-85c0-269d51d0e83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255324} {:answer/id #uuid "be619077-ef60-58f6-93be-483e6d9cbf83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255369} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ce09ca-94dc-54bf-a873-caeb486ff0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967cb4cb-b2bc-5b09-a044-cead97fab3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177502} {:answer/id #uuid "124e4ecc-80db-5945-b2a0-0a70bacf7766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177503} {:answer/id #uuid "35a85fe9-c0c0-5c48-acbe-861e57fb6043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177504} {:answer/id #uuid "637fe0b8-5f7e-55d6-8cf4-e9808987ffcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{213}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177505} {:answer/id #uuid "707fa4c1-45e1-573b-8f56-1b0a414f3bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177506}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0597155-ae64-5f5d-9a35-8dbfb090d8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177501}] :question/id #uuid "a4f83414-82d1-57f1-a541-5f71790a0a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122579" :question/problem "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds$,\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47302108-d687-5b9f-8466-13bda0aa9a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254273} {:answer/id #uuid "635dfe49-19a8-5b28-a7d6-0119af1c3aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254563} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7f9994-8ead-51fc-a0bc-e35e1c013a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254628} {:answer/id #uuid "843a82b2-282f-5e4f-91da-3c42e53d7862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(36-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254846} {:answer/id #uuid "8acd42aa-8188-53b4-8b19-2defe96db9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8acd42aa-8188-53b4-8b19-2defe96db9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0aed9aa-ec0d-5834-81f4-f0a6213310d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9c9eb7b-4691-5649-a35d-df87aec5b233" :question/math-academy-id "q-122574" :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244cbd35-4706-5f3b-a48a-e1061c8c3156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253996} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad97c8-3c56-5c9a-a8c2-77eadd3dfd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254080} {:answer/id #uuid "378b4c07-594e-5889-86f8-ae72d53a2f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254151} {:answer/id #uuid "941292e1-b3c5-55ef-a3f3-007f8b0b0228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254972} {:answer/id #uuid "f98e4257-7cc8-5955-bed8-55177dfe7d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98e4257-7cc8-5955-bed8-55177dfe7d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3046664-9e6f-53e3-9c0f-658dd83eef2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7198c6d2-e4a6-5178-afaa-7f9ff2209737" :question/math-academy-id "q-122593" :question/problem "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b6c179-73bb-5998-9a66-e7ee5c7354ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272594} {:answer/id #uuid "31820ed3-8684-5823-b207-558c302546c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272830} {:answer/id #uuid "48925fc8-d494-54fd-9553-6cb85067e122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} {:answer/id #uuid "604802b6-34cb-5971-9a07-92a58988eaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273218} {:answer/id #uuid "70fe7ed9-a4bf-5385-8fdc-2d4b842e7f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48925fc8-d494-54fd-9553-6cb85067e122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e949706-b44d-5eac-8496-453a3fc3a2e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9463919f-5148-546d-ad7d-95a446b32afe" :question/math-academy-id "q-149727" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c557a-d0d0-5ebb-811c-2baf6a3cefd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272593} {:answer/id #uuid "781b70bc-38cd-5810-ad6e-167d9400e156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "d73b4363-3c1d-563d-8b69-1e8275c1c9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3eabed-9398-5497-8cc2-1d7f384966fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274495} {:answer/id #uuid "f863851f-99c9-59c9-9adb-eb61d4881fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d73b4363-3c1d-563d-8b69-1e8275c1c9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} :answer-field/id #uuid "c355ca0d-7553-591c-b07c-62d0f6a0048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b09217dd-ddfe-59be-95cc-af28d1d6d361" :question/math-academy-id "q-149745" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad4d22b-acab-5ed4-80d7-49438eaa6ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519948} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a9fe0e-461d-5c50-9b13-e2d874c7c501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519960} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b8d36-7fec-572a-aff6-b1b470283083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519963} {:answer/id #uuid "dede3d58-8509-5b02-a30e-5c2edec92aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{285}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519972} {:answer/id #uuid "e96d81fd-09d1-5c57-816e-ef56ea09fc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e96d81fd-09d1-5c57-816e-ef56ea09fc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519976} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a780e2-ad8c-5ca4-b690-f0fe637d9239" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "316743f9-ae6e-5a5c-90d0-c46ad39f440f" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3f6e03956e6002db05950b10d9608be87dd45a4bb75b3822e1ab754981eb19d.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}2 x y^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(\\frac{1}{2},2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}})^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x y^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) t^{6} \\\\  & =2 t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 x y^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{5}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=t^{4} + 1,$ $\\text{d}u=4 t^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{2}^{17}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(17\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{17 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502b4fef-e465-5b61-8882-5e374dab527e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519931} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa47a8c-d91a-57c1-a9f1-0922f42018fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519934} {:answer/id #uuid "68c61336-093f-55bd-aa10-7226bb03c3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3} + 4 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519941} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff5fdaa-596f-587d-a87a-1b719d485ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3} + 4 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519956} {:answer/id #uuid "d906e193-9c9a-5192-b2b6-679b2f764aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c61336-093f-55bd-aa10-7226bb03c3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3} + 4 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9402cd-0a21-588e-9736-a8a0d09641c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5487c7b6-44fe-5e39-9226-bf0a43081f99" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=e^{\\frac{y}{2}}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=e^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨e^{\\frac{t}{2}},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{\\frac{t}{2}})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{e^{\\frac{t}{2}}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{e^{\\frac{t}{2}}}{2})^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 \\left(e^{\\frac{t}{2}}\\right)^{6} e^{- 2 t} \\\\  & =2 e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 x^{6} e^{- 2 y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{e^{t}}{4} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\sqrt{e^{t} + 4} e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=e^{t} + 4,$ $\\text{d}u=e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\sqrt{e^{t} + 4} e^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =1{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[1\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(6\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4 \\sqrt{6}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{10 \\sqrt{5}}{3} + 4 \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1462cb59-c704-506e-a83c-16cf44ae2975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519917} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4517a3-aa53-5919-9bed-4810f2d2c48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519927} {:answer/id #uuid "4c732881-a009-564d-95a7-a2eeeff3e247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519928} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7575b3-0dca-5cc3-83db-5f2b03ab84a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{123}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519930} {:answer/id #uuid "528322e2-0164-531d-815c-501f2c5a4e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "528322e2-0164-531d-815c-501f2c5a4e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519932} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e62414b-49f9-5db8-a201-3192213542b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5b75b6c1-d336-52ca-ae2f-f314c60fb3a9" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $y^2=6x$ from $(\\frac{7}{6},\\sqrt{7})$ to $(\\frac{8}{3},4).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny^2=6x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t^{2}}{6},\\,t⟩,\\,\\sqrt{7}\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $\\sqrt{7}\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t^{2}}{6})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{t}{3})^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{6 x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{6 \\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{6}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\left(t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2}}{9} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\frac{t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=t^{2} + 9,$ $\\text{d}u=2 t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{7}}^{4}\\frac{t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{16}^{25}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right]_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(25\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(16\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{125}{9}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{9}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b01bf31-2ee2-53d0-94d2-836a1979c75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519915} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7f8963-5894-537f-beb8-fd372b21c68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519923} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c1f173-d6f1-508b-9bb0-95bc71c51c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519961} {:answer/id #uuid "df9f311c-5c28-5284-b186-7d31aaae46f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519973} {:answer/id #uuid "f537f4b8-eb49-5ef0-9790-4395f8071591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9f311c-5c28-5284-b186-7d31aaae46f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6400f1c6-0b7b-5401-8a4b-73a82dadcf86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757a5531-af3b-53d8-af0b-e619bd9a8745" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 1$ from $(5,2)$ to $(7,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 1,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 t \\\\  & =2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[\\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{3^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =5 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39dca523-9171-5e40-833c-978d9ad71849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519922} {:answer/id #uuid "473858c3-157d-5055-9739-f0d6b85e7989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519925} {:answer/id #uuid "754a7457-a3e8-51f7-b689-9b4291afc217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519943} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa6f2cf-f8b0-51f7-9093-58c3d7bf3ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519953} {:answer/id #uuid "fe15649c-aa4e-5adc-af44-f12a4b3fb802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe15649c-aa4e-5adc-af44-f12a4b3fb802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519982} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cb8063-be0e-59e9-b0ab-6ae36122c5ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a06b7a8-a31a-5d91-a4c2-27f8e724e7b7" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 1$ from $(5,2)$ to $(11,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 1,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2 t \\\\  & =2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[\\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{5^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{25}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =21 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498a3dd0-f17b-5f70-8b03-fc510f9aad6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{152 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519926} {:answer/id #uuid "97f57c54-8701-5d8a-a5ed-fd3c0f65306b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519951} {:answer/id #uuid "af54eed5-bbc0-5fe0-84c5-eb933f76c6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519964} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ae83d0-87f4-5859-85f3-d0abf6c9594d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519968} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9120ba-23f5-5da5-845b-c9f43ed0780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff9120ba-23f5-5da5-845b-c9f43ed0780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519983} :answer-field/id #uuid "a078cda7-6548-5368-8ce0-29f35e1abda9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c77817c-c092-573a-a129-dda1b7dbaf00" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 t \\\\  & =8 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\left(8 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[4 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{3^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =20 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ee718e7-8c66-5b5c-9686-158a4a052a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2} + \\frac{343}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519937} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2f283d-ca77-5bd5-b76f-fd081e1ef092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{2} - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519942} {:answer/id #uuid "985323ac-4ffe-5a8b-b35b-a8ae16606764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519952} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec560c5-8a2a-5832-803c-a59a7a317dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519955} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5db845-80f2-5dbc-b2a3-2f9cc3faee50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d2f283d-ca77-5bd5-b76f-fd081e1ef092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{2} - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519942} :answer-field/id #uuid "64569d65-6b5b-57eb-a178-6af1a5ac90dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1335390-7338-553c-9752-14a0d9faa957" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca91ec7d1e638b49ab7d4334397c5c7d383b7df0bce022c0e481b857ae2e1b95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(12,2 \\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{3}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2 t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{6 x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{6 \\left(t^{2}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =6 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{6 x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}\\left(6 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}6 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1,$ $\\text{d}u=8 t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}6 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{5}^{49}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{49}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(49\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{343}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{343}{2} - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d5f72a-c449-56eb-989f-22226e0b50de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519914} {:answer/id #uuid "128d4ac3-24ee-55ce-9b25-4cf80792cec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519916} {:answer/id #uuid "7d024a42-dbb0-595d-9286-4d48315042fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519945} {:answer/id #uuid "a286ac33-a522-5b67-b3fa-62a475c8f733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519959} {:answer/id #uuid "b4231752-2303-5f37-a885-fec04df72753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a286ac33-a522-5b67-b3fa-62a475c8f733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519959} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aabe041-866e-5d3f-b02e-f537a8abdf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d33ab010-ae47-54a5-a66e-bc5313115fb2" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(4,1)$ to $(6,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6 t \\\\  & =6 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(6 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[3 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{2^2}{2}-\\frac{1^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6 \\sqrt{5}\\left(2-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =9 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1baa52c1-5c71-5f87-8551-3279611003dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519919} {:answer/id #uuid "67732790-16a3-5527-b750-580381646c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519940} {:answer/id #uuid "8e21c30b-33aa-5878-9f58-a724973bf4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "116 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519950} {:answer/id #uuid "9d078917-01b7-5c9d-8b60-6b5248691b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519954} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9d7d72-6508-5e36-8e89-fed8dd873be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "312 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d078917-01b7-5c9d-8b60-6b5248691b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519954} :answer-field/id #uuid "afcc0b52-3f45-5cf3-a53f-e03a34d67289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edc5b731-7331-5410-b96a-68df46082b51" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(12,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 t \\\\  & =8 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\left(8 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[4 \\sqrt{5} t^{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{5^2}{2}-\\frac{2^2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\sqrt{5}\\left(\\frac{25}{2}-2\\right) \\\\  & =84 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16637d83-9d55-58dd-9a6e-b892dcb60c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519918} {:answer/id #uuid "2aae3009-c21c-529f-9656-63ab4497ccd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519920} {:answer/id #uuid "d57a9be7-b91b-5c57-b613-1fb34ee7dd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{32}{5} + 160 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519970} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b390e9-d955-54c3-bde9-22bd5a28116c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5120}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519975} {:answer/id #uuid "f5509479-ec88-5363-9bf0-f7d33b99fdee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16637d83-9d55-58dd-9a6e-b892dcb60c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519918} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f6ae96-8b2e-581c-93a9-a1569f0c7cc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f20094c6-0c28-54cf-af5d-3c0a666f7927" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad80637c8c61ee847bd385c39fb0eec5fc1b14ee4f9d6c1796fb31baff48c016.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 \\sqrt{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(2 \\sqrt{5},5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 \\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 \\sqrt{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 5$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\sqrt{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=8 x y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8 \\left(2 \\sqrt{t}\\right) t \\\\  & =16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\left(16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}16 t \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4 t + 4,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}16 t \\sqrt{t + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & =8{\\int }_{8}^{24}\\frac{u-4}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{24}\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2} - 2 \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2}{5}u^{5/2}-(4)\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right)\\right]_{8}^{24} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5} - \\frac{4 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{8}^{24} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(24\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(24\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(8\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(8\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{1152 \\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- 64 \\sqrt{6}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{128 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{64 \\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{832 \\sqrt{6}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}]}
